Welcome to notebook nine. Real code is full of operations that might not produce a value. A map lookup may miss. A string may fail to parse. A network call may fall over. In java or python the usual answers are null, thrown exceptions, and sentinel values. Three different mechanisms, all of them invisible to the type system. This notebook introduces the three Scala types that turn each of those situations into an ordinary value, so the compiler can then force you to handle the missing or failed case before you can use the result.

The three types are Option, Try, and Either. Option of A handles absence. Either there is an A inside, or there is not. Try of A handles failure as exception. Either you got an A, or some throwable was raised. Either of E and A handles failure as data. Either you got an A on the right, or an E on the left describing what went wrong.

All three are sealed two case enumerations under the hood. So everything you learned about pattern matching in the previous notebook applies directly. The new skill on top is combinators. Methods like map, flat map, get or else, fold, and recover, that let you transform the value inside the container without ever writing an if x not equal to null check.

Before we dive into each one, notice they share a structure. Each is a container of zero or one element, and each has two cases. Option of A is either Some of a or None. Try of A is either Success of a or Failure of throwable. Either of E and A is either Right of a or Left of e. On the success side they each hold one value. On the failure side, Option holds nothing, Try holds a throwable, and Either holds whatever error type E you chose. That difference, how much you know about why it failed, is the whole basis for choosing between them.

Let us start with Option. The cure for null. A reference that might be null is a value the type system lies about. The compiler tells you it is a String, but at runtime it might not be. Every reader of that code has to remember to null check, and forgetting is a bug. Option of A lifts the absence into the type. A value of type Option of String is honest. It announces at compile time that you must consider both possibilities.

There are three ways to construct an Option. Some of x wraps a value you know is present. None is the absent case. And Option of x, used as a smart constructor, returns Some of x if x is non null, or None if x is null. This third form is the bridge from any java api that may hand you a null. You wrap the result in Option, and from that point on the type system carries the absence information for you. Many standard library operations already hand back an Option directly. The biggest one you will meet daily is the get method on Map, which returns Some of the value if the key is present, or None if it is not.

The most direct way to take an Option apart is pattern matching. A match on a name of type Option of String, with case Some of n, arrow, hello followed by n, and case None, arrow, hello stranger, handles both cases explicitly. Because Option is a sealed two case enum, the compiler checks exhaustiveness. Omit the None branch and you will get a warning that the match is not exhaustive. Exactly the property that makes null so dangerous, fixed at the type level.

Pattern matching works, but for one liners there are two combinators that read better. The first is get or else. It provides a default for the absent case. So ages dot get of alice dot get or else zero returns thirty if alice is present, or zero if not. The second is fold. Fold takes two argument lists. The first is the value for the empty case, the second is a function from the present value to the result. So ages dot get of alice dot fold of unknown, applied to a function age to age is age, returns the string age is thirty if alice is present, or the string unknown if not. Fold is the right tool when the success and absent cases produce different shapes of computation, but the same final type.

One important warning. Avoid calling dot get on an Option. The dot get method throws no such element exception when the Option is None. That defeats the whole point of having an Option in the first place. If you find yourself reaching for it, you almost always wanted get or else, fold, or pattern matching instead.

Often you do not want to unwrap an Option yet. You want to transform the value inside and stay in the container until the end of the pipeline. Three operations let you do that. The map method, applied to a function f, returns Some of f of a if the Option was Some of a, or stays None if the Option was None. The flat map method is like map, but the function f itself returns an Option. This avoids ending up with Some of Some of something. And the filter method, applied to a predicate p, keeps the value if it was Some and the predicate is true, and otherwise turns it into None.

The reason flat map matters so much is that real code chains several may fail operations, where each step depends on the previous one. Written with explicit flat map, the code gets nested and drifts to the right. The for comprehension turns the same logic into a linear shape. Imagine you want to look up a user id by name, then look up their profile by id, then read the email from the profile. Each step returns an Option. With nested flat map, you write idByName dot get of name dot flat map of, then a lambda taking id and calling profileById dot get of id dot flat map of, then a lambda returning the email. With a for comprehension, you write for, id arrow idByName dot get of name, profile arrow profileById dot get of id, email arrow profile dot email, yield email. Same logic, linear shape. The first None at any step short circuits and the whole expression becomes None.

The for body desugars to exactly the flat map and map chain you just saw. Every left arrow becomes a flat map, except the last one, which becomes the map produced by the yield. Once you internalise this, the same shape works for Try, for Either, for Future, and for any other type that has compatible flat map and map methods. The for comprehension is the universal sequencing tool in Scala.

Now to the second type. Try. Try turns exceptions into values. Some operations can fail by throwing. Parsing a string. Dividing by zero. Calling out to a java api. Try of A wraps a block of code, runs it inside its own try catch, and gives you back either Success of a or Failure of throwable. Where Option answers, is there a value, Try answers, did this throw, and if so, with what. You import it from scala dot util dot Try.

Writing Try of the expression quote forty two quote dot to int returns Success of forty two. Writing Try of the expression quote oops quote dot to int returns Failure containing a number format exception. Writing Try of ten divided by zero returns Failure containing an arithmetic exception. Crucially, the block inside Try is evaluated lazily, by name. The Try call sees the unevaluated expression and runs it inside its own try catch. That is why the divide by zero does not blow up at the call site. It is captured.

Pattern matching a Try has the same shape as Option. Two cases. Case Success of n, arrow, the string parsed followed by n. Case Failure of e, arrow, the string failed followed by e dot get message. Exactly like Option, the compiler enforces both cases.

Try has the same map and flat map you saw on Option. The new combinator on Try is recover. Recover converts a Failure into a Success by handling specific exception types. It is like a catch block, but as a value level operation. You write the recover method with a partial function. For example, recover of case underscore colon number format exception, arrow, minus one. If the Try was Failure with a number format exception, it becomes Success of minus one. If it was Success already, recover does nothing. There is also recover with, which is to recover what flat map is to map. The recovery function itself returns a Try, which lets you retry an operation by yielding another Try inside the handler.

One important constraint. Try only catches non fatal throwables. Things like out of memory error, stack overflow error, and interrupted exception are considered fatal. They indicate the java virtual machine or thread is in serious trouble and should propagate up to the runtime. This is the right default. You should not be writing application code that swallows an out of memory error. Treat Try as a value shaped replacement for try catch around expected failure modes. Parsing. Input output. Third party calls. For control flow that branches on a known reason, prefer Either, which is coming up next.

One small bridge worth knowing. If you do not care why something failed, only that it did, you can collapse a Try into an Option with the dot to option method. The throwable inside the Failure is dropped on the floor, and Failure becomes None. This is the right move when you want to chain a Try result into a pipeline of Option operations.

Now the third type. Either. Failure as data. Try is convenient when failure means some exception got thrown. But often failure is expected, and you want to give it a name. User not found. Invalid email. Insufficient funds. Those are not exceptions in the runtime sense. They are business outcomes that the type system should make visible. Either of E and A has two cases. By convention, Right carries success, the value of type A. Left carries failure, the value of type E, which is often your own error enum. The mnemonic is right is right.

You construct an Either with Right of forty two, or Left of the string missing value. You pattern match it with case Right of n, arrow, the string got followed by n. Case Left of e, arrow, the string err followed by e. Same shape as Option and Try. The big win, though, comes when E is your own enum. You enumerate the known failure modes, and the compiler then enforces exhaustiveness wherever you handle them.

Consider a signup function. Define an enum called SignupError with three cases. Empty name. Invalid email, carrying the offending string. And already registered, carrying the email. Then write a signup method returning Either of SignupError and User. If the name is empty, return Left of empty name. Else if the email does not contain an at sign, return Left of invalid email carrying the bad value. Otherwise return Right of the new User. Now any code that consumes that result has to handle every named error. Add a new case to SignupError, and the compiler will point out every match expression that is no longer exhaustive. The same change impact analysis property you saw with pattern matching, now applied to your error types.

Modern Scala treats Either as right biased. Which means map, flat map, and for, all operate on the Right side. A Left short circuits the whole chain, just like None does for Option. So Right of ten dot map of plus one becomes Right of eleven. Left of nope dot map of plus one stays Left of nope. There is also a fold method on Either. Fold of, function from e to result, function from a to result, collapses either side into a single value. Useful when you finally need to render the outcome.

Sequencing multiple Either returning steps with for works the same way as Option. The first Left short circuits and is returned as the result of the whole comprehension. There is one important constraint though. All the steps must use the same E error type for the for to compile cleanly. If one step returns Either of String and Int, and another step returns Either of My Error and Int, you will need to align them, usually by mapping the left side with the dot left map method or by widening to a common supertype. We will see more disciplined ways to handle this when we get to error handling and resources in notebook fourteen.

Worth knowing in passing. The dot to int option method on String is the Option returning version of dot to int. It returns Some of the integer if parseable, or None otherwise. No exceptions involved. And the dot to right method on Option converts an Option into an Either by supplying the Left value for the None case. Its sibling dot to left does the inverse. Together they are the bridge between absence shaped failure, which is Option, and named failure, which is Either.

So how do you choose between the three. There is no rule the compiler enforces, but there is a clear convention. Use Option when absence is the only failure and no reason is needed. Use Try when wrapping code that throws and you just want the value or the exception, without caring what kind. Use Either when failures are part of your domain, when the kind of failure matters to the caller, and you want to give those failures names. Concretely, a map get is Option, because missing key needs no further explanation. A call into a java parser is Try, because anything could be thrown and you do not care to enumerate it. A signup flow with five named rejection reasons is Either of SignupError and User, because the kind of rejection matters to the caller.

You will routinely cross between the three. Here are the standard conversions. From Option to Either, use dot to right, passing the value to put in the left case. Or dot to left, passing the value to put in the right case. From Try to Option, use dot to option. From Try to Either, use dot to either. That gives you Right of the success value, or Left of the throwable. From Either to Option, use dot to option, which drops the Left and returns Some or None. Notice that the direction that loses information is always available freely. The direction that adds information, like Option to Either, requires you to supply the missing piece, which is exactly what dot to right does.

Let us close with a small pipeline that uses all three. Reading a config value. The value may be missing, which is Option shaped. Parsing it as a number may throw, which is Try shaped. Validating it against a business rule may reject, which is Either shaped. Define a ConfigError enum with three cases. Missing carrying the key. Not a number carrying the key and the bad value. Out of range carrying the key and the integer. Then write a read int method returning Either of ConfigError and Int. Inside, use a for comprehension. First, raw arrow config dot get of key dot to right of missing key. This gets the raw string or fails with missing. Second, n arrow Try of raw dot to int dot to option dot to right of not a number. This parses the raw string into an integer, or fails with not a number. Finally, yield n. To validate that the port is in a sane range, write a read port method. Inside another for, get n by calling read int of port, then check the range using Either dot cond of, the test n greater than zero and less than sixty five thousand five hundred thirty six, the right value unit, and the left value out of range carrying port and n. Yield n.

Read that pipeline top to bottom and notice what is not there. No null checks. No try catch. No thrown exceptions. Every may fail step is a value in the type. Every transition is a left arrow inside the for. And every failure is an instance of a named enum that the consumer must handle exhaustively. This is what idiomatic Scala error handling feels like once the three types are in your hands. By the way, Either dot cond, that helper used above, is a tiny utility that builds a Right or a Left from a boolean test. It saves writing if test then Right something else Left something.

Notebook ten zooms out to generics and variance. What the bracket A in Option of A actually means. How Option of Cat relates to Option of Animal. And how to write your own generic containers and functions. After that you will be equipped to reason about every parameterised type in the standard library, not just the three you met here.
